---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import { guides } from '../../data/guides';

const guide = guides.find((g) => g.slug === 'cannot-delete-branch-used-by-worktree')!;
const toc = [
  { id: 'short-answer', label: 'The short answer' },
  { id: 'what-it-means', label: 'What the error means' },
  { id: 'find-the-worktree', label: 'Find the worktree' },
  { id: 'check-nothing-is-lost', label: 'Check nothing is lost' },
  { id: 'remove-then-delete', label: 'Remove it, then the branch' },
  { id: 'deleted-by-hand', label: 'Deleted by hand' },
  { id: 'ai-agents', label: 'Why AI agents leave these' },
  { id: 'many-worktrees', label: 'When there are dozens' },
];
---
<Guide guide={guide} toc={toc}>
  <Fragment slot="checked">
    Every command and message below was run in a throwaway repository on git 2.54.0 (Apple Git-157), with the folder path shortened to <code>~/code</code>.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>
  <p>
    Git will not delete a branch while any worktree has it checked out. Find that worktree with
    <code>git worktree list</code>, check that it holds nothing you need, remove it with
    <code>git worktree remove</code>, and then delete the branch with <code>git branch -d</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-it-means">What the error means</h2>
  <p>
    A worktree is an extra working folder attached to the same repository, so you can have several branches checked out
    at once. Git remembers which branch each worktree has checked out, and it refuses to delete a branch that is in use,
    because that would pull the ground from under that folder. The wording depends on your version of git:
  </p>
<pre class="term"><code><span class="p">$</span> git branch -d claude/checkout-redesign
<span class="e">error: cannot delete branch 'claude/checkout-redesign' used by worktree at '~/code/web/.claude/worktrees/checkout-redesign'</span></code></pre>
  <p class="term-note">
    Older versions of git say <code>Cannot delete branch 'name' checked out at 'path'</code>. The cause and the fix are the same.
    The message already names the folder, which is the one thing you need for the next step.
  </p>

  <h2 id="find-the-worktree">Step 1: find the worktree that holds the branch</h2>
  <p>
    <code>git worktree list</code> shows every working folder attached to the repository and the branch each one has checked out.
  </p>
<pre class="term"><code><span class="p">$</span> git worktree list
~/code/web                                      651badc [main]
~/code/web/.claude/worktrees/checkout-redesign  1615dbb [claude/checkout-redesign]</code></pre>
  <p>
    The first line is your main checkout. Every other line is a linked worktree. A worktree whose folder has gone missing
    is marked <code>prunable</code>, and one that was locked on purpose is marked <code>locked</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="check-nothing-is-lost">Step 2: check that nothing is lost</h2>
  <p>
    Removing a worktree deletes its folder, so look before you remove anything. There are two things that can exist
    only there.
  </p>
  <h3>Commits that are not in your main branch</h3>
<pre class="term"><code><span class="p">$</span> git log --oneline main..claude/checkout-redesign
1615dbb redesign checkout</code></pre>
  <p>
    If this prints nothing, every commit on the branch is already in <code>main</code>. If it prints commits, they are
    not merged yet. They may also be on a remote, which <code>git branch -vv</code> shows, or they may exist nowhere
    else. Do not delete the branch until you know which.
  </p>
  <h3>Files that are not committed</h3>
<pre class="term"><code><span class="p">$</span> git -C ~/code/web/.claude/worktrees/checkout-redesign status --short
?? notes.txt</code></pre>
  <p>
    Empty output means the worktree is clean. Anything listed, here an untracked <code>notes.txt</code>, exists only in
    that folder. Git protects you from the worst of this: it refuses to remove a worktree that has such files.
  </p>
<pre class="term"><code><span class="p">$</span> git worktree remove .claude/worktrees/checkout-redesign
<span class="e">fatal: '.claude/worktrees/checkout-redesign' contains modified or untracked files, use --force to delete it</span></code></pre>
  <p>
    Read that message as a stop sign, not a hurdle. The <code>--force</code> option deletes those files for good.
  </p>

  <h2 id="remove-then-delete">Step 3: remove the worktree, then the branch</h2>
  <p>Once the worktree is clean, remove it. A successful removal prints nothing.</p>
<pre class="term"><code><span class="p">$</span> git worktree remove ~/code/web/.claude/worktrees/checkout-redesign</code></pre>
  <p>
    Now the branch is free. <code>git branch -d</code> only deletes a branch whose commits are already merged, so if the
    work was never merged, git tells you so:
  </p>
<pre class="term"><code><span class="p">$</span> git branch -d claude/checkout-redesign
<span class="e">error: the branch 'claude/checkout-redesign' is not fully merged</span>
<span class="d">hint: If you are sure you want to delete it, run 'git branch -D claude/checkout-redesign'
hint: Disable this message with "git config set advice.forceDeleteBranch false"</span></code></pre>
  <p>
    That is the check from step 2 happening again, and it is the one to take seriously. Merge the branch, push it
    somewhere, or decide the work is not needed, and then delete it. After the branch was merged into
    <code>main</code>:
  </p>
<pre class="term"><code><span class="p">$</span> git branch -d claude/checkout-redesign
Deleted branch claude/checkout-redesign (was 1615dbb).</code></pre>
  <p>
    The hash in that last line matters. If you delete a branch by mistake, <code>git branch claude/checkout-redesign 1615dbb</code>
    puts it back, for as long as git has not cleaned up the unreachable commits.
  </p>

  <h2 id="deleted-by-hand">If you deleted the worktree folder by hand</h2>
  <p>
    Deleting the folder in Finder or the terminal leaves git's record of the worktree behind, and the branch stays locked
    to it. The worktree shows up as <code>prunable</code>:
  </p>
<pre class="term"><code><span class="p">$</span> git worktree list
~/code/web   1615dbb [main]
~/code/wt2   1615dbb [feature/two] prunable

<span class="p">$</span> git worktree prune -v
Removing worktrees/wt2: gitdir file points to non-existent location</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>git worktree prune</code> only forgets worktrees whose folders are gone. It never touches a folder that exists,
    and it does not delete the branch, so run <code>git branch -d</code> afterwards. One warning: a worktree on an
    external drive that is not plugged in also looks gone. Plug the drive in before you prune, or git forgets a worktree
    that is perfectly fine.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai-agents">Why AI coding agents leave these behind</h2>
  <p>
    Coding agents use worktrees so several tasks can run on one repository without disturbing each other. Claude Code can
    work in a worktree it creates inside the repository, under <code>.claude/worktrees/</code>. Cursor, Codex and
    Conductor keep theirs in <code>.cursor/worktrees</code>, <code>.codex/worktrees</code> and
    <code>conductor/workspaces</code> according to their documentation, so check where your own install puts them. Branch
    names often start with <code>claude/</code>, <code>cursor/</code> or <code>codex/</code>, which is a quick way to
    recognise them in <code>git branch</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Depending on the tool and on how a run ended, a worktree is sometimes cleaned up for you and sometimes left behind:
    runs that made changes, runs that crashed and runs you abandoned. Each one is a full copy of your files, and if the
    agent installed dependencies there it is a copy of <code>node_modules</code> as well, so a pile of them takes real disk
    space. They also hold exactly the branches that git then refuses to delete.
  </p>

  <h2 id="many-worktrees">When there are dozens of them</h2>
  <p>
    Two or three worktrees are easy to handle with the steps above. Twenty means running the same checks twenty times,
    and the cost of getting one wrong is a folder of uncommitted work that is gone. That is the case Lopper was made for,
    and it is described below.
  </p>
</Guide>
